1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,075
Welcome to Module Nine.

2
00:00:02,075 --> 00:00:05,350
You know the classic example of a fixed action pattern.

3
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:09,412
A goose sees an egg outside the nest and rolls it back with its bill.

4
00:00:09,412 --> 00:00:14,812
The sequence runs the same way every time, even if you take the egg away halfway through.

5
00:00:14,812 --> 00:00:18,725
It is cheap, fast, and reliable in the world it evolved for.

6
00:00:18,725 --> 00:00:21,062
Flexible foraging is the opposite.

7
00:00:21,062 --> 00:00:25,037
The animal samples, adjusts, and decides patch by patch.

8
00:00:25,037 --> 00:00:27,975
It copes with surprises, but it costs more.

9
00:00:27,975 --> 00:00:29,987
Most animals use both.

10
00:00:29,987 --> 00:00:32,862
And when you build with language models, you should too.

11
00:00:32,862 --> 00:00:37,000
Yesterday you built an agent that decides its own next step.

12
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,087
Today's question is when to let it decide at all.

13
00:00:40,087 --> 00:00:43,750
Anthropic draws a clear line between two kinds of system.

14
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:49,387
In a workflow, you write the sequence of steps in code, and the model fills in each step.

15
00:00:49,387 --> 00:00:53,100
In an agent, the model decides the sequence itself.

16
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:55,637
And their main advice is almost blunt.

17
00:00:55,637 --> 00:00:58,025
Find the simplest solution that works.

18
00:00:58,025 --> 00:01:01,925
Add complexity only when it clearly improves the results.

19
00:01:01,925 --> 00:01:04,650
Sometimes that means no agent at all.

20
00:01:04,650 --> 00:01:07,325
They describe five workflow patterns.

21
00:01:07,325 --> 00:01:11,637
A chain, where one step feeds the next, with checks in between.

22
00:01:11,637 --> 00:01:15,787
A router, which sorts each input to a specialised path.

23
00:01:15,787 --> 00:01:22,837
Parallel work, either splitting a job into independent pieces, or running the same job several times and comparing.

24
00:01:22,837 --> 00:01:28,075
An orchestrator, which breaks a job down on the fly and hands out the pieces.

25
00:01:28,075 --> 00:01:33,637
And an evaluator, where one model drafts and another critiques, round after round.

26
00:01:33,637 --> 00:01:37,900
Agents are for open-ended problems, where you cannot predict the steps.

27
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:44,862
They are powerful, but they cost more, they vary from run to run, and their mistakes can build on each other.

28
00:01:44,862 --> 00:01:48,562
There is also evidence that fixed pipelines can hold their own.

29
00:01:48,562 --> 00:01:59,087
One well-known pipeline with three fixed steps, find the problem, repair it, check it, was reported to be competitive with free-roaming agents on a standard coding benchmark.

30
00:01:59,087 --> 00:02:01,675
So where does the metaphor fail?

31
00:02:01,675 --> 00:02:04,600
The goose did not choose its egg-rolling routine.

32
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:09,887
Selection fixed it over countless generations, and running it costs nothing extra.

33
00:02:09,887 --> 00:02:15,475
You choose, today, for each task, and flexibility is paid for on every single run.

34
00:02:15,475 --> 00:02:19,875
And unlike the goose, you can open up a workflow and test each step.

35
00:02:19,875 --> 00:02:23,737
That inspectability is often the real reason to prefer one.

36
00:02:23,737 --> 00:02:27,887
In today's lab you will run a literature-triage task two ways.

37
00:02:27,887 --> 00:02:31,875
You will make twenty short abstracts with an answer key you check yourself.

38
00:02:31,875 --> 00:02:38,887
Then you will triage them with a fixed chain, extract, check, decide, and with a free agent that plans its own approach.

39
00:02:38,887 --> 00:02:43,887
You will compare accuracy, time and number of calls, and then read every mistake.

40
00:02:43,887 --> 00:02:47,750
Notice how much easier it is to find where the chain went wrong.

41
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:51,125
Open your terminal, and let's compare repertoires.

